<section class="section method" aria-labelledby="method-title"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / Behind the work</p><h2 id="method-title">A running game.<br/>A visible process.</h2><p>The Godot development harness grew out of making A Household Exorcism. It connects agent implementation to the running game, captures what happened, and keeps verification tied to the build being tested.</p><p>Agents help implement and exercise the systems. Human direction and hands-on play decide whether the result works as a game.</p><a class="text-link" href="/harness/">Inside the development harness →</a></div><div class="terminal"><div class="terminal-head">AE / DEVELOPMENT LOOP</div><ol class="workflow"><li><div><strong>Direct</strong><span>Design intent, scoped work, written decisions.</span></div></li><li><div><strong>Build</strong><span>Agent implementation and independent review.</span></div></li><li><div><strong>Observe</strong><span>Player input, screenshots, recorded evidence.</span></div></li><li><div><strong>Verify</strong><span>Regression checks, build receipts, human QA.</span></div></li></ol><p class="terminal-foot">↳ Iterate from what the game actually does.</p></div></section>
<section class="section"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Selected industry credits</p><div class="credits-intro"><h2>Independent work.<br/>An industry foundation.</h2><p>Before and alongside Aesthetic Engine: production and QA across established studios, shipped games, and demanding release cycles. These are professional team credits, separate from the independent projects above.</p></div><Credits/><div class="section-end"><a class="text-link" href="/about/">More about Matt →</a><a class="text-link" href="https://www.mobygames.com/person/315620/matt-munroe/credits/">Full credits on MobyGames ↗</a></div></section>
